00:00:05.150 --> 00:00:09.490
Hello, and welcome back to another video
with ItsDrDan, and today we're going to

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:12.150
be looking at specific heat capacity.

00:00:12.730 --> 00:00:16.520
So for this particular video, we're going
to go into a short, sweet definition

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:18.549
of what specific heat capacity is.

00:00:18.890 --> 00:00:23.660
and how you can use it to calculate heat
and some very common type of problems

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:28.480
that you'll use in introductory general
chemistry to how to use this value.

00:00:28.889 --> 00:00:33.349
So whenever specific heat is used, this
is always something that is referred

00:00:33.349 --> 00:00:39.840
to as the amount of heat required to
increase something by one degree Celsius.

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:45.070
Now, when we're raising that temperature,
this is for one gram of a substance.

00:00:45.545 --> 00:00:50.375
So, specific heat is unique for every
single material that you would come

00:00:50.375 --> 00:00:52.714
across in the lab or in your home.

00:00:53.085 --> 00:00:55.824
Every single thing takes
so much energy to heat up.

00:00:56.230 --> 00:01:00.960
So the way that we like, I like to look at
this is think of it is there are numbers

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:03.760
that are assigned for these, these are,
these are things that have been measured

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:08.679
within laboratory settings and the whole
idea is if I look at both of these two

00:01:08.679 --> 00:01:14.129
substances, I have water on the right
hand side, I have iron on the left.

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:17.320
So what exactly will it do?

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:20.520
So if I apply, let's say the
same amount of heat to both.

00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:22.670
Well, which one's going to heat faster?

00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:26.670
So one way to think about this is if
you put, let's say you put water on the

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:31.440
stove to make, let's say pasta or rice,
or you're just trying to boil it to eat.

00:01:31.929 --> 00:01:35.349
And one thing you're going to realize
is like, Oh man, this is taking so long.

00:01:35.349 --> 00:01:37.479
I just want this to boil faster.

00:01:37.759 --> 00:01:42.589
It usually can take anywhere from five to
maybe 15 minutes, depending on your stove.

00:01:42.589 --> 00:01:46.229
It takes a very, very long
time for that to boil.

00:01:46.509 --> 00:01:49.050
And that's because it's
absorbing all of that energy.

00:01:49.675 --> 00:01:53.405
from the fire in order to
eventually reach boiling.

00:01:53.725 --> 00:01:57.925
So now if you can look here,
water has a specific heat of 4.

00:01:57.955 --> 00:02:02.284
186 and that's joules
over grams per Celsius.

00:02:02.575 --> 00:02:05.304
So the amount of joules, so 4.

00:02:05.335 --> 00:02:12.015
186 joules is required to heat
up one gram by one single degree.

00:02:12.015 --> 00:02:12.385
Okay.

00:02:12.385 --> 00:02:14.235
So that's a lot of energy for each time.

00:02:14.595 --> 00:02:17.205
Now you don't usually just
put water directly on a stove.

00:02:17.205 --> 00:02:17.455
Sure.

00:02:17.455 --> 00:02:21.665
Maybe in a laboratory setting, you might
put in a beaker, but at home, usually

00:02:21.665 --> 00:02:26.124
the way that you cook things is using
like, uh, maybe cast iron pans or maybe

00:02:26.124 --> 00:02:30.895
aluminum pans, some sort of metal you're
applying directly to that, to the burner.

00:02:31.455 --> 00:02:35.620
Now, When you put that on the burner,
does it get hot quickly, right?

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:39.120
So at first it's very cold, but
within probably about five to 10

00:02:39.130 --> 00:02:42.600
seconds, you can already start
feeling the heat getting absorbed

00:02:42.749 --> 00:02:44.520
within that piece of metal, right?

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:47.819
And now you can really see that
with that lower value here, right?

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:49.330
0.

00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:49.560
451.

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:52.870
That's a lot less energy that's
required to heat that up.

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:54.860
So it's going to be a lot less effort.

00:02:55.185 --> 00:02:57.685
For the heat to be able to do that.

00:02:58.165 --> 00:03:00.905
So that's kind of the idea here of
what we're going to be looking at.

