This is the SI unit for energy.

00:04:15.939 --> 00:04:21.370
So we have joules now We have M which
you've seen in many different probably

00:04:21.370 --> 00:04:22.940
chemistry videos all over the internet.

00:04:23.150 --> 00:04:24.430
That is our mass, right?

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:29.170
So we are going to be measuring that in
grams So we have joules is equal to grams.

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:33.260
Now C, this is our specific heat, right?

00:04:33.419 --> 00:04:34.979
Where do we get specific heat from?

00:04:35.219 --> 00:04:37.349
Well, it's a specific heat capacity.

00:04:37.570 --> 00:04:42.829
So that capacity part where that C
comes from, that is what that is for.

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:47.960
So the units for that are
joules over grams by Celsius.

00:04:48.295 --> 00:04:50.945
So you can already see that the
grams are going to cancel between

00:04:50.945 --> 00:04:52.675
mass and the specific heat.

00:04:53.015 --> 00:04:56.965
Okay, so a lot of times when we talk about
specific heat, we'll abbreviate that as

00:04:57.025 --> 00:05:02.335
SH, just so that way we're not having
to constantly write what that value is.

00:05:02.955 --> 00:05:07.674
Now delta T, this is our
change in temperature.

00:05:08.205 --> 00:05:14.115
Okay, so when we talk about delta
T, this is always about the final

00:05:14.125 --> 00:05:16.885
temperature minus the initial.

00:05:16.915 --> 00:05:19.125
Okay, so this is a little
formula for yourself.

00:05:19.385 --> 00:05:24.175
If you see a delta, that's always
final minus initial, and that's going

00:05:24.175 --> 00:05:26.555
to be reported in units of Celsius.

00:05:27.015 --> 00:05:31.674
So Celsius is going to be used in this
calculation, um, and this is generally

00:05:31.674 --> 00:05:33.025
what our units are reported in.

00:05:33.525 --> 00:05:34.905
So let's take a look at this.

00:05:34.905 --> 00:05:39.474
Now, when we are using this formula,
there's usually two types of problems

00:05:39.925 --> 00:05:45.215
that you will generally see, um, when you
are doing these types of calculations.

00:05:45.585 --> 00:05:49.325
The first one is usually you're
calculating the amount of heat

00:05:49.415 --> 00:05:54.055
that is required either to heat
something or to cool something down.

00:05:54.475 --> 00:06:00.094
So heating is generally, it will be
a positive or a positive value when

00:06:00.094 --> 00:06:02.104
you are heating it for your Q value.

00:06:02.125 --> 00:06:05.805
So you'll, your joules would be
positive or that's endothermic.

00:06:06.105 --> 00:06:11.075
If it's a negative value, that's referring
to exothermic or it's releasing heat.

00:06:11.415 --> 00:06:14.875
Right, so sign is going to be really
important in these calculations.

00:06:15.205 --> 00:06:17.925
So you always want to make sure
you're paying careful attention.

00:06:18.635 --> 00:06:21.225
Now the second type of
problem is identifying an

00:06:21.245 --> 00:06:23.745
unknown metal, or substance.

00:06:23.754 --> 00:06:28.214
Being that specific heat is unique
for every single material, we

00:06:28.214 --> 00:06:30.634
can use this to identify things.

00:06:31.045 --> 00:06:33.875
So let's try to take a look
at each of these problems.

00:06:34.145 --> 00:06:37.475
So that way we can get an
idea of what it is that we're

00:06:37.475 --> 00:06:38.405
going to be seeing with them.

00:06:38.945 --> 00:06:39.425
All right.

00:06:39.425 --> 00:06:42.975
With calculating specific heat
questions, the big thing about these,

00:06:42.975 --> 00:06:47.165
they tend to be word problems, meaning
that we need to make sure we are very

00:06:47.214 --> 00:06:51.505
organized and how we are writing all
the little pieces that are in a problem.

00:06:51.905 --> 00:06:53.285
So let's take a look at this one.

00:06:53.715 --> 00:06:57.095
So it says how many
kilojoules of heat are lost?

00:06:57.325 --> 00:07:00.635
Okay, so right off the rip,
it's telling you exactly what it

00:07:00.635 --> 00:07:02.155
